chapterNumber: 7,
chapterName: "Quality Assurance and Management",
vivaQuestions: [
{ question: "What is Software Quality Assurance (SQA)?", answer: "Sir, SQA is a planned and systematic set of activities that ensure the software processes and products conform to established requirements and standards." },
{ question: "What is the difference between QA and QC?", answer: "Sir, Quality Assurance (QA) is process-oriented and focuses on preventing defects. Quality Control (QC) is product-oriented and focuses on identifying defects in the final product." },
{ question: "What are Quality Concepts?", answer: "Sir, quality concepts refer to the attributes of software that measure its excellence, like reliability, maintainability, usability, and performance." },
{ question: "What is an FTR?", answer: "Yes sir, FTR stands for Formal Technical Review. It is a class of review conducted by software engineers to uncover errors in function, logic, or implementation." },
{ question: "What is the primary objective of an FTR?", answer: "Sir, the main objective of an FTR is to find errors early in the development lifecycle so they can be fixed before they become expensive to resolve." },
{ question: "Who is involved in an FTR?", answer: "Sir, an FTR typically involves a Review Leader (moderator), the Producer (author of the work), and several Reviewers." },
{ question: "What is Software Reliability?", answer: "Sir, software reliability is defined as the probability of failure-free operation of a computer program in a specified environment for a specified time." },
{ question: "What does ISO 9000 standard specify?", answer: "Sir, ISO 9000 is an international family of standards that outlines criteria for a quality management system, ensuring organizations meet customer and regulatory needs." },
{ question: "What is CMM?", answer: "Sir, CMM stands for Capability Maturity Model. It is a framework used to assess and improve the maturity of an organization's software development process." },
{ question: "How many levels are there in CMM?", answer: "Sir, CMM consists of 5 maturity levels: Initial, Repeatable, Defined, Managed, and Optimizing." },
{ question: "What is the 'Initial' level in CMM?", answer: "Sir, at level 1 (Initial), processes are ad hoc, chaotic, and heavily dependent on individual heroics rather than standard procedures." },
{ question: "What is Six Sigma for Software Engineering?", answer: "Sir, Six Sigma is a data-driven methodology aimed at eliminating defects and reducing variation in processes to achieve near-perfect quality." },
{ question: "What is an SQA Plan?", answer: "Sir, an SQA plan is a document that identifies the SQA activities, standards, guidelines, and procedures to be followed throughout the software project." },
{ question: "What is defect prevention?", answer: "Sir, it is a proactive approach in QA where root causes of past errors are analyzed to improve processes so those errors do not occur again." },
{ question: "Are reviews considered testing?", answer: "Sir, yes, reviews are considered 'static testing' because they evaluate the work product without actually executing the code." },
{ question: "What is 'Conformance to Requirements'?", answer: "Sir, it is a definition of quality meaning the software accurately does what the specified requirements (SRS) state it should do." },
{ question: "How is Software Reliability measured?", answer: "Sir, it is often measured using mathematical and statistical models based on historical failure data, like Mean Time Between Failures (MTBF)." },
{ question: "What is the 'Optimizing' level in CMM?", answer: "Sir, Level 5 (Optimizing) is the highest level, where the organization focuses on continuous process improvement through quantitative feedback." },
{ question: "Why are FTRs restricted in time and participants?", answer: "Sir, keeping meetings under two hours and involving only 3-5 people ensures the review remains focused and productive without wasting resources." },
{ question: "What is a baseline in quality management?", answer: "Sir, a baseline is a fully reviewed and approved work product that serves as a foundation for further development and can only be changed through formal procedures." }
],
